.\" cadapt(1) — the adapt self-adaptive lossless codec, in C.
.\" Authored by Pedro Anisio de Luna e Silva (pedroanisio@faz.ai), 2026-09-15,
.\" with Claude Opus 5 (Claude Code). Every figure is the binary's own
.\" (cadapt --help, cadapt info) or a measurement named in LOCKS.md and
.\" docs/performance-audit-128mib-2026-09-15.md. Render: man -l cadapt.1
.TH CADAPT 1 "2026-09-15" "cadapt 0.12.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cadapt \- compress and decompress files and folders with the adapt context-mixing codec
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cadapt encode
.I input
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-verify " | " \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-explain ]
.RB [ \-\-fast " | " \-\-mid " | " \-\-max ]
.RB [ \-0 " .. " \-11 ]
.RB [ \-\-allocation
.BR auto | window | context ]
.RB [ \-\-contexts
.BR auto | lean ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-segment\-bytes
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-adaptive
.BR rates | sparse | retained | auto ]
.RB [ \-\-transform
.BR bmp | text | none ]
.br
.B cadapt decode
.I input
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-output
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-memory
.IR bytes ]
.RB [ \-\-threads
.IR n ]
.br
.B cadapt info
.I file
.br
.B cadapt \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cadapt
is a lossless compressor of the context-mixing kind: every bit of the input
is predicted by a set of adaptive context models whose predictions are
combined by learned arbiters and refined by estimation stages, then coded
by a binary arithmetic coder.
Decoding runs the same model in the same order, so the decoder needs the
same memory the encoder used.
.PP
Two independent choices decide what a run does, and they are easy to
confuse:
.TP
.B the profile
.RB ( \-\-fast ,
the default, or
.BR \-\-max )
selects
.I which model
runs: how many contexts, how many inputs per context, how many arbiters and
estimation stages.
A bigger model writes fewer bytes and takes longer, whatever its memory.
.TP
.B the memory tier
.RB ( \-0 " .. " \-11 )
selects
.I how much memory
that model may fill.
The model shrinks or grows its context table, recall table and history
window to fit; the profile's fixed tables are the same at every tier.
More memory buys ratio, not speed.
The tier is encode's only memory setting, and the file records the model
it produced: whoever decodes the file needs that model's memory, so the
tier is a promise about every decoder, not only about this run.
.PP
So
.B \-9 \-\-max
is the max model in 674 MiB (the default budget, so
.B \-\-max
alone means the same),
.B \-5 \-\-max
is the same model squeezed into 94 MiB, and
.B \-9
alone is the normal model in 674 MiB.
The two axes are independent: no tier changes the model's shape, no
profile changes the memory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI encode " input"
Compress
.I input
to
.IR input .adpt
(or to
.BR \-o ).
A named file or folder is stored with its metadata (modes, timestamps;
for folders every entry, hard links, holes, extended attributes and ACLs).
.I input
may be
.B \-
for standard input, in which case the container is a bare stream with no
metadata.
.TP
.BI decode " input"
Restore the file or folder
.I input
holds, to the name with
.B .adpt
stripped (or
.B .out
appended), or to
.BR \-o .
The model is allocated as the container's header names it, whatever the
decoder's own settings.
.TP
.BI info " file"
Print the container's format version, its declared size, the model's
sizes (table, recall table, window, class history, contexts, profile), the
transforms it carries, what it holds and the memory decoding it takes.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH PROFILE OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-fast
Two hashed contexts chosen per file, a direct order-1 memory, the word run,
recall and one arbiter; a table of at most 2 MiB.
Decodes about three times faster than the normal profile for about a
twelfth more bytes.
Container version 20 (23 with metadata).
.TP
(default)
The normal profile: ten hashed contexts with one input each, two run
models, four arbiters, two estimation stages.
Version 19 (22 with metadata); 84 (85) when its second, third and
fourth arbiters take the class or recall keys the first block prefers,
as under
.BR \-\-max .
.TP
.B \-\-mid
The normal profile's tables and estimation stages with seven hashed
contexts fixed by the profile (orders 2, 3, 4 and 6, word, record and
the class history, whose width is still chosen per file) and its first
arbiter alone as the verdict.
Measured against the normal profile: on the first 8 MiB of enwik8
1.3 % more bytes for 37 % fewer encode cycles and 25 % fewer decode
cycles; on enwik8 1.4 % more bytes (18,761,302 against 18,500,473) in
29 % less encode time and 24 % less decode time; on enwik9 2.4 % more
(172,472,731 against 168,451,193) in 28 % and 26 % less. 2.1 to 2.4
times faster than
.BR \-\-max ,
for 4.8 % more bytes on enwik8 and 17.5 % on enwik9.
Version 76 (78 with metadata; 77 and 79 under the text transform).
Not with
.BR \-\-adaptive ", " "\-\-transform bmp" " or " "\-\-contexts lean" .
.TP
.B \-\-max
Twenty hashed contexts with three inputs each, six run models, five
arbiters, a second estimation stage four times the normal one.
Writes fewer bytes for about 1.8 times the time in the same memory.
The second, third and fourth arbiters take one of three key policies,
chosen per file on the first block: the classic keys (the previous byte,
the order-2 hash, the word so far; version 72, 75 with metadata), the
byte classes with the nesting state (80, 82) or the classes with recall
(81, 83); info names the choice as
.IR "arbiter keys" .
Versions 21 and 24 are read.
.TP
.BR \-\-adaptive " " rates | sparse | retained | auto
Experimental causal predictors layered over the normal or max profile;
.B auto
spools the input to temporary disk once, tries the base and the three
adaptive profiles (and the BMP transform where it applies) and keeps the
smallest complete container that fits the budget, using up to eight
encodes.
Versions 25 to 52.
.TP
.BR \-\-transform " " bmp
Code a BMP24 image through reversible colour and row residuals.
.TP
.BR \-\-transform " " text | none
The text transform: a word both sides have seen written out three times
becomes, from then on, a code of one to three bytes the model reads as
one symbol. Each side builds the same bounded book as the file is coded;
nothing is sent ahead and nothing is built in, and every other byte
passes as it is. A file of 8 MiB or more whose first block looks like
text takes it by itself;
.B text
asks for it whatever the input (a pipe's length is unknown, so a pipe
gets it only when asked), and
.B none
refuses it. Measured on enwik8 at 128 MiB, 3.5 % fewer bytes in fewer
seconds; below 8 MiB it costs, which the rule is for. Not with
.B \-\-adaptive
or
.BR "\-\-transform bmp" .
Versions 62 and 63 ahead of the normal and fast profiles, 67 and 68 with
metadata; the max profile under the transform is its sized version 72
(75), the sizing byte naming the transform and the arbiters' 1/32
starting weight, which the transform earns (3,336,047 to 3,330,628 bytes
on the first 16 MiB of enwik8 at 128 MiB); 64 to 66 and 69 to 71 are
read, no longer written.
.SH MEMORY OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-0 " .. " \-11
The tier:
.B \-0
to
.B \-9
are what
.BR xz (1)
takes to compress at the same preset, so a user of xz reads them as
before;
.B \-10
and
.B \-11
are the two tiers above xz's ladder, for the max profile's largest tables
and a window the size of a 1 GB input:
.TS
l l l l l l l.
preset	-0	-1	-2	-3	-4	-5
MiB	3	9	17	32	48	94
.TE
.TS
l l l l l l l.
preset	-6	-7	-8	-9	-10	-11
MiB	94	186	370	674	1024	2048
.TE
.IP
.B \-9
is the default; the last tier given wins.
A tier below the profile's smallest model is raised to that model, never
refused.
The tier bounds the model, its window and the workspace; a folder's path
and metadata bookkeeping is additional.
There is no byte-count budget on
.BR encode :
.B \-\-max\-memory
belongs to
.BR decode ,
where it is a cap, and is refused on
.BR encode .
.TP
Floors
The smallest model of the fast and normal profiles takes 28,178,567 bytes
(about 27 MiB), the max profile's 73,283,719 (about 70 MiB), so
.BR \-0 " to " \-3
all mean the 27 MiB floor and, under
.BR \-\-max ,
.BR \-0 " to " \-4
all mean the 70 MiB one.
.B \-\-verify
alongside the coder needs two models.
.TP
Ceilings
The context table stops growing at 2^26 blocks (1 GiB), the recall table
at 2^24 entries (64 MiB), the window at 2^30 bytes; the fast profile's
table at 2 MiB.
On enwik8 (100 MB) the max profile is saturated at
.BR \-9 :
.B \-10
and
.B \-11
write the same bytes, the table being at the input's target.
.TP
What a tier buys
Measured on enwik8 (100 MB) with
.BR "\-\-max \-\-allocation context" ,
commit 89e2486, 2026-09-15 (a dated table; the locked floors below are the
figures a run verifies):
.TS
l r r r.
tier	context table	container	vs \-9
_
\-5, \-6	36 MiB	19,252,902	+5.7 %
\-7	128 MiB	18,744,782	+3.0 %
\-8	256 MiB	18,451,834	+1.3 %
\-9	512 MiB	18,207,203	\(em
\-10, \-11	512 MiB	18,207,203	0
.TE
.IP
At
.B \-5
(raised to the smallest max model) and
.B \-6
the table is bought with the compact side tables, the lean context set
and a fraction past 2^21 blocks, under the text transform (the sized
version 58, 61 with metadata, its sizing byte carrying all four); at
.B \-7
with the compact tables (the text version 65, 70 with metadata); from
.B \-8
the full tables win (64, 69).
.TP
Locked floors
.\" floors:start
Locked 2026-09-16 on AMD Ryzen 5 5600X 6-Core Processor,
.BR "cadapt encode --max --allocation context --contexts lean" ;
a change may only improve on these:
.TS
l r r r.
fixture	input	container floor	time floor
_
enwik8-8MiB	8,388,608	1,669,716	10.7 CPU s
enwik8-16MiB	16,777,216	3,236,535	19.8 CPU s
enwik8	100,000,000	17,800,141	107.4 CPU s
enwik9	1,000,000,000	145,784,134	1,054.0 CPU s
.TE
.\" floors:end
.TP
.BR \-\-allocation " " auto | window | context
How a budget is spent once the smallest model fits:
.B window
grows the history window first, then the context table;
.B context
grows the table first;
.B auto
(the default) grows the table first only where the window-first sizes would
leave it below its target for the input — an input longer than the window
the budget allows, whose table then stays at its floor — and is the
window-first sizes everywhere else. Under
.B \-\-max
it also halves the side tables (versions 53/56) when the budget would
otherwise stop the table at 2^22 blocks or fewer, short of the input's
target, and spends the difference on a table twice the size, with a lean
context set (54/57) while the table stays at that size; and what the
budget still holds once the window has grown goes to the table an eighth
of its next doubling at a time (58/61), never at the window's expense.
A table at the input's target takes no fraction, so the default budget's
containers are unchanged. The rule fires under every profile at the tiers
whose window cannot hold the input: on the first 16 MiB of enwik8,
.B \-\-fast \-0
writes 3.7 % fewer bytes than window-first,
.B \-\-max \-1
12 % fewer and
.B \-\-max \-6
1.4 % fewer, at the same peak memory and the time of
.BR "\-\-allocation context" ;
.B \-\-mid
and the normal profile size alike either way there.
Either can win depending on the input; on enwik8 at
.B \-9
(commit 89e2486)
.B context
writes 18,207,203 bytes and
.B window
18,393,652.
.TP
.BR \-\-contexts " " auto | lean
Which context set the model runs:
.B auto
(the default) chooses it by coding the first block under each candidate;
.B lean
takes the lean set outright — word and field left out and, under
.BR \-\-max ,
o8, word3 and wordc too (the sizing byte's lean bit, written with the
full side tables as well since this option; a reader older than it
refuses such a file). Time for bytes on text: the measurements are in
LOCKS.md. Not with
.BR \-\-fast .
.TP
Decoding memory
.B decode
allocates the model the container's header names, so a file written at
.B \-9
takes about 640 MB to decode on any machine (for the max profile; about
370 MB for the normal one), and a file written at
.B \-11
up to 2 GiB.
.BI \-\-max\-memory " bytes"
on
.B decode
only caps that: a container needing more is refused with the bytes it
needs, never shrunk; without it nothing is refused.
.SH OTHER OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Where to write; existing files are kept unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.B \-
is standard output (encode) or standard input (decode).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing output.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-output " bytes"
On decode, refuse a container that declares more decoded bytes than this.
For folders the cap is on the combined logical sizes of restored files,
including sparse holes and each hard-link alias; a refused entry discards
the staging directory and leaves the existing destination intact.
Without it nothing is refused: the container alone decides what decoding
writes (see FMT-6 in
.IR docs/CONSTRAINTS.md ),
and a service that decodes untrusted containers sets the cap itself.
.TP
.BR \-\-verify ", " \-\-no\-verify
.B encode
skips round-trip verification by default.
.B \-\-verify
proves the output as it is written when the budget holds two models, on a
second thread; otherwise, for a file written to a file, in a second pass
with one model.
.B \-\-no\-verify
states the default.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Adds to the report, on standard error, what the first block decided: the
tables and the allocation taken, the class-history width, context set,
record period and arbiter keys, the sample codings that chose them and
their bytes, and whether the text transform is on. The container is the
same with or without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " n"
With
.I n
of two or more,
.B encode
cuts the input into segments of
.B \-\-segment\-bytes
(default 16777216, whole blocks of 262144) that each start the model
afresh, and codes them at once on as many of the
.I n
threads as the budget holds a model, a segment and a block's workspace
for, and never more than the CPUs online;
.B decode \-\-threads
decodes a segmented file the same way.
The sizes stay fitted to the
.I n
asked for, so the bytes are the same on every machine; the host only
decides how many workers code them at once, and a request past its CPUs
is noted.
Each restart costs ratio (about +4 % on enwik8 with 16 MiB segments), and
each worker needs a whole model, so within a small budget one thread is the
better answer.
The report says how many threads ran.
.TP
.BI \-\-segment\-bytes " bytes"
The segment length for
.BR \-\-threads .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ADAPT_ISA
Set to
.B baseline
to run the portable per-bit path instead of the x86-64-v3 one on a CPU
that has it.
Both write the same bytes; the knob exists so the two can be compared.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The command succeeded.
.TP
1
It failed: an input that could not be read or decoded, an output that
could not be written, a budget below the model's floor, a container the
decoder's cap refuses.
Nothing is left half-published.
.TP
2
A usage error; the usage text follows the message.
.SH EXAMPLES
The floor configuration, the max model in the default 674 MiB with the
table grown first:
.PP
.nf
    cadapt encode enwik8 --max --allocation context
.fi
.PP
The same model in 94 MiB, and what it will take to decode:
.PP
.nf
    cadapt encode enwik8 --max --allocation context -5
    cadapt info enwik8.adpt
.fi
.PP
The normal model in 32 MiB, which the max model cannot enter:
.PP
.nf
    cadapt encode data.bin -3
.fi
.PP
The max model in 2 GiB, the top tier, for a 1 GB input; and a decoder on
a 1 GiB machine refusing it with the bytes it needs:
.PP
.nf
    cadapt encode enwik9 --max --allocation context -11
    cadapt decode enwik9.adpt --max-memory 1073741824
.fi
.PP
A folder, proven as it is written, and restored:
.PP
.nf
    cadapt encode project/ -o project.adpt --verify
    cadapt decode project.adpt -o restored/
.fi
.PP
A stream through a pipe:
.PP
.nf
    tar c src | cadapt encode - --fast > src.tar.adpt
    cadapt decode - < src.tar.adpt | tar x
.fi
.SH FILES
Containers carry a format version in their header; a decoder refuses a
version it does not implement.
Versions 13 to 18 are refused; 19 to 24 are the three profiles as streams
and as named files or folders; 25 to 52 are the experimental profiles;
53 and 56 are the max profile with compact side tables, 54 and 57 with
its lean context set as well; 58 and 61 the max profile sized to its
budget, one byte after the header naming the side tables, the context
set, the table's fraction, the text transform, the arbiters' 1/32
starting weight and, in bit 7, the second max model (int16 arbiters,
four re-purposed context slots, four-way recall buckets, a fifth
arbiter). Current max encodes use 72 (75 with metadata), identifying the
pressure-gated model2 policy; 21, 24, 53, 54, 56 and 57 remain readable; 62 to 66 are the text transform ahead of the normal,
fast, max, compact and lean models, 67 to 71 with metadata, of which the
max ones (64 to 66, 69 to 71) are read and no longer written; 76 and 77
are the mid profile, plain and under the text transform, 78 and 79 the
same with metadata; 80 and 81 are the sized max profile of 72 with its
second, third and fourth arbiters keyed by the byte classes and the
nesting state (80) or by the classes and recall (81), a choice the
encoder makes per file on the first block, 82 and 83 the same with
metadata; 84 is the normal profile of 19 with the same key policies,
one policy byte after the header naming the policy and the text
transform, 85 the same with metadata.
For historical 58/61 model2 files below a table of 2^21 blocks, the decoder
checks both historical predictors sequentially, using temporary disk for
a consumed input prefix and tentative output. It emits bytes only after
checksum verification and keeps one model in memory.
.PP
On POSIX, file outputs use a private 0700 staging wrapper and retained file
and directory handles for metadata, publication and cleanup. Deferred folder
and root attributes are kept in an automatically removed temporary journal,
replayed after checksum verification with bounded memory. A temporary-storage
failure discards staging and preserves the existing destination.
